NOTE : For a verilog verification run the option "all", the simulator may have
       problems with allocating memory, due to the large size of the stimulus
       reader file. This problem has been observed on both LDV and Modelsim.
       The make options Vic2, Vic3 may create the same problem as
       mentioned above for Modelsim (verilog).

       The Error message displayed for each simulator is as follows:

       Modelsim
       ========
       # Note: Significant memory allocation has occurred in this simulation
       #  - Setting the LockedMemory variable in your .ini file may help
       # performance
       # ****** Memory allocation failure. *****
       # Please check your system for available memory and swap space

       LDV
       ===
       Data structure takes 927447416 bytes of memory
       Error!    No more memory available (allocating 16376
       bytes)                                            [Verilog-NOMEM]
       "../../bustest/invec/bif.sim", 558605:

       TESTS
       =====
       1. VHDL
          ====
          All tests can run in VHDL. 

       2. VERILOG
          =======
          Using ModelSim -> All tests can run except "Vic2", "Vic3" & "all".
          Using LDV      -> All tests can run except "all" option.

================================================================================

Steps to create .bif and .sim formatted vectors from the BusTalk 
code (default)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
make <testname>

This will create infile.bif and bif.sim in the ./invec directory

Please refer to the 'makefile' for legal 'testname's. 

RTL simulations
---------------
The following directive in the Vic.h file should be set as follows:
#define VICrtl_sim 1

Netlist simulations
-------------------
The following directive in the Vic.h file should be set as follows:
#define VICrtl_sim 0

NOTE : Option "all" for make option may create memory allocation problem
       during simulation as large number of vectors are loaded.
       For verilog simulation with modelsim, the make options Vic1, Vic2 and
       Vic3 may create the same problem as mentioned above.

